at's where the plotly api comes in  so plotly has implementations in python  as well as r  there's a little bit of a learning curve  to it because it's different from  working just with ggplot2  uh but you can sort of take your  visualizations to the next level  so this is just a gallery of plotly  examples  uh here's an example of what some of the  different code will look like  as you can see it is overall pretty  similar to ggplot2 code  because there is kind of a grammar of  graphics framework underlying it  but you can just do more with plotly  with  a visualization that's been created  using the plotly api  so for example here notice that i'm  hovering over these points here  and it's telling me the x and y  coordinates then i can also do things  like  let's suppose i want to zoom in in  instances where the sepal length is  greater than  six i don't care about smaller than that  i can do things like  let's just zoom into these data points  up here  notice i've zoomed into that aspect of  the graph  i care about that more i can just see  that in greater detail  that gives me as the end user a lot more  um i can just do a lot more with it  maybe i want to double click here  because then i want to go back  so it's just a different style of  creating graphs than what you can get  from ggplot2 alone  that i highly do recommend learning once  you're familiar with ggplot2 of course  alright so we've covered all the  packages for your data visualization  needs  now we're going to move on to packages  that are going to help you deal with  somewhat problematic data types and data  structures  and now i'm going to go through these a  little bit faster just because  honestly there's not quite as much to  say for them  but package number eight is going to be  per r  so first of all the package does have an  awesome name  but even beyond that what it's going to  help you to do  is a couple different things number one  it's going to help you apply  functions to multiple different elements  of some structure  so think of it as an alternative to  creating a for loop where you're doing  the same thing  many times or even as an alternative to  the built-in  apply family of functions so we have  this  map family of functions which  these functions can be used to help us  return a list  or a character vector or a double vector  so on and so forth  but the primary function of parar  is helping us work with lists so we can  do things like filter them  and reshape them summarize join them  lists are a very flexible data structure  in r but i understand for beginners  they can be a little bit  counterintuitive and certainly have a  learning curve to them  and per r is really going to help you  with that  moving on to package number nine that's  string r  this one is pretty self-explanatory it's  going to help you deal with strings  so again let's go over to the cheat  sheet here  this does a really good job of laying  out all the capabilities of this package  things like detecting matches subsetting  strings  managing the lengths of strings mutating  them  joining them everything that you can  pretty much  regular expressions pretty much  everything that you can imagine  i have a tutorial all about uh string r  that i'll have in the description  then package number 10 is lubridate  because  honestly date times uh they can be even  more problematic and painful  even than strangs those of you who  disagree with that statement i just made  have obviously never dealt with  uh daylight savings time date times and  r  it can get it can get just incredibly  painful  so things like getting and setting the  components  extracting the various components  of date times and then just doing math  with your date times with  these various objects you've got periods  durations and intervals so all these  different  classes and data types that are going to  help you uh just work  work with date times and specifically  the differences between various date  times 