ill see all kinds of things that  you never even knew were possible using  r at all but  hey if you master shiny you are a very  very powerful programmer that's all i'm  gonna say  package number 15 here is going to help  you take your more bare bones basic  shiny apps to the next level  and that's shiny dashboard so shiny  dashboard is going to shine when you  have  a shiny app that you can sort of boil  down to three distinct parts  a header a sidebar and a body  and even from this most basic dashboard  that you see  here this is a little bit nicer looking  than your basic shiny app  because you have a lot of nicer looking  more professional colors going on here  but shiny dashboard is just going to  give you a lot more  options for controlling the look and the  feel  and even some other functionality for  behavior  of what your application's actually  going to look like  we're saving some of the most fun topics  for last  package number 16 is going to help you  with all of your machine learning needs  and that's carrot so i just got done  doing a four part tutorial series on  carrot  because there is so much functionality  out of this package  basically everything that you could need  to do in a machine learning pipeline  whether that's things like actually  creating the data partition  so that you have a training set and a  testing set  pre-processing your data creating uh  some kind of training control  actually doing the training summarizing  your results  all of that is embedded here inside of  carrot it's  one package that has all this different  stuff all within one single package that  yeah it has a learning curve but once  you learn it  it's extremely powerful now with this  list  with specifically with number 17 i'm  going to cheat a little bit here because  for the most part i've tried to isolate  this to  singular packages whereas this package  here  tidy models it's not really one  package so much as it's a bundle of  incredibly small packages  so with carrot you have one single  package that has  probably literally hundreds of different  functionalities  whereas with tidy models you've got all  these  really small packages which each serve  distinct purposes  so things like r sample that's different  resampling approaches  that's a package recipes that's your  transformations for  pre-processing that's a package parsnip  is a package for  model creation yardstick for measuring  your model performance  so it's two very different approaches  for machine learning  now where i think that this is going is  four years from now  i think tidy models is gonna have a lot  of development  uh at this time that i'm recording this  video anyway  a lot of these packages aren't  completely mature and there's things  changing with them all the time  carrot is more mature but i do think  over the long run  things are going to go the way of of  tidy models  i will say too with tidy models  syntactically  i think it's a little bit more user  friendly and has less of a learning  curve  than carrot does so keep that in mind as  well  package number 18 is going to be for all  of your deep learning needs  and that's an implementation of the  keras api  so in full disclosure i am not a deep  learning expert by any stretch of the  imagination  however what i'll say is as easy as it  is going to be  the keras package is going and r is  going to make that for you  so there's an example here on the  vignette for keras where it kind of  breaks down an example for you but you  can do things like just defining your  model  and then using the same a sort of tidy  framework  adding layers to it you've got  functionality built in for summarizing  compiling the model specifying things  like your loss function  uh your optimizer your metrics of  interest  fitting the model the plotting  functionality is built in there  evaluation all that all that wonderful  stuff  so keras is the foundation for this book  uh deep learning with r it's actually  co